# Loom — top-precedence project rules

This file is loaded for every provider family (Anthropic, OpenAI/Codex,
Gemini, anything else) and wins on conflict with any other rules file.
Keep it short, provider-neutral, and focused on invariants that hold
regardless of which model is driving.

## What this project is

A VS Code extension that provides an AI coding agent. Three runtimes:

1. **TypeScript extension host** (`src/`) — VS Code Node environment;
   owns anything that touches the VS Code API (editor edits, terminal,
   diagnostics, webview lifecycle).
2. **React webview** (`webview-ui/`) — Vite build rendered in a VS Code
   webview; talks to the host via `postMessage` only.
3. **Go agent binary** (`agent/`) — spawned as a child process; owns
   the agent loop, LLM client, pure-Go tools, and MCP client. Talks to
   the extension over stdio with LSP-framed JSON-RPC 2.0.

Wire types live in `src/shared/protocol.ts`. Any change to a message
type must be mirrored on both sides.

## Architectural invariants — do not violate without discussion

- **Go owns the agent loop.** Do not move agent logic into TypeScript.
  The TS side is a proxy.
- **TS owns the VS Code API.** Go never calls VS Code APIs directly;
  if it needs editor state, it asks via a `tool.call` RPC.
- **Webview is dumb.** It renders state and forwards user input. No
  business logic, no LLM calls, no file system access.
- **JSON-RPC framing is LSP-style** (`Content-Length` header + body)
  for the extension↔agent bridge. Newline-delimited JSON is only used
  inside `agent/internal/mcp/` for external MCP servers.
- **Prompt prefix must stay byte-stable.** The system prompt is split
  into a stable prefix (mode + tools + skills catalogue) and a volatile
  tail (workspace path + loaded-skill bodies + rules bundle). Anything
  per-turn-variable goes in the tail. Tool list order is deterministic
  (MCP tools sorted by name). Breaking byte-stability breaks the
  Anthropic and OpenAI prompt caches.
- **Parallel tools are the default.** The loop dispatches concurrent
  tool calls via an errgroup (cap 8). Approvals batch into a single
  `tool.approveBatch` RPC.
- **Search-first, read narrowly.** Use `search` (content grep) and
  `find_files` (filename glob) before reading. `read_file` supports
  `offset`/`limit` and soft-caps very large files; do not slurp whole
  files when a slice is enough.
- **`apply_diff` over whole-file rewrites.** Two edit shapes — anchor
  `{oldText, newText}` for small unique regions, range
  `{startLine, endLine, newText}` for known line spans. On any
  failure, use `search` to find the right slice and re-issue a tight
  range edit. Never re-emit the whole file as a workaround.

## Tool conventions

- `newText` is always a JSON string. For multiline content use literal
  `\n` between lines. To delete, pass an empty string `""`. Never pass
  `null`, an array, or an object.
- Approvals: any write or shell command requires approval; pure reads
  do not.
- Background work goes through `run_command_background` +
  `read_process_output` + `kill_process` — `run_command` is one-shot
  with a ≤120 s cap.

## Project context loading

Loom only reads `.loomrules` (this file) for project rules, plus
`.loom/skills/`, `.loom/agents/`, and `.loom/commands/` for skills,
sub-agent presets, and slash commands. Foreign-format files
(`CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`, `GEMINI.md`, `.claude/`, `.codex/`,
`.gemini/`, `.cursor/`, `.cursorrules`, `.github/copilot-instructions.md`,
`.github/instructions/`) are not loaded. Workspaces that want to share
content across tools should symlink or generate `.loomrules` from
another file.

## Build, test, ship

- `npm install` (also installs Husky pre-commit hooks).
- `npm run build` — full build (TS extension + webview + Go agent).
- `npm run test:ts` — vitest for the TS side.
- From `agent/`: `go test ./...` and `go vet ./...`.
- Pre-commit hook: when staged changes touch `src/`, `agent/`,
  `webview-ui/src/`, `package.json`, `scripts/`, or
  `.github/workflows/`, at least one of `README.md`, `CLAUDE.md`,
  `AGENTS.md`, `.github/copilot-instructions.md` must also be staged.
  Skip with `SKIP_DOCS_CHECK=1` only for genuinely doc-free changes.

## Style

- TS: strict mode; `unknown` + narrowing over `any`; `node:` prefix on
  built-in imports; `await` over `.then()`; named exports.
- Go: `gofmt` clean; `go vet` clean; wrap errors with
  `fmt.Errorf("context: %w", err)`; prefer channels over shared state;
  keep SDK-specific types inside `internal/llm/`.
- React: function components only; `useState`/`useReducer` (no state
  library); VS Code CSS variables for theming.

## Cross-cutting

- Cross-platform paths: `filepath.Join` in Go, `path.join` from
  `node:path` in TS. Never string-concatenate paths.
- Use `stderr` for Go logging — stdout is the RPC channel.
- Webview CSP is strict: no inline scripts; bundle through Vite.
